<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV Probus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1258–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry IV the Righteous</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV Probus was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> from 1270 and High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> from 1288 to 1290. Raised at the Bohemian court, he was a patron of German poetry and wrote verse in Middle High German himself. He sought a royal crown for Poland, but died young, possibly poisoned, and his ambitions passed with his lands to rival princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus.jpg" alt="Henry IV Probus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qkiel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryk_Probus,_orygina%C5%82,_Wroc%C5%82aw_1a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
